From dc343c675da5206c1d10b4b0007635e1dd7d9cf8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gemini Mainline Experiment <gemini-mainline@example.invalid>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 21:19:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 153/156] wifi: mt6797: accept userspace scan elements the
 passive scan never transmits

The standard supplicant attaches an extended-capabilities element to
every scan request it issues. The wiphy registers a zero scan-element
limit, and with a hw_scan operation mac80211 leaves it at zero
("userspace will not be allowed to in that case"), so cfg80211 rejects
the request before the driver sees it and the supplicant loops on a
failed scan trigger.

Register a bounded limit of 256 bytes instead; mac80211 subtracts the
room its own elements need. The one admitted scan is passive with no
SSID, no flag, no duration and one channel, so no probe request is ever
transmitted and the elements are never read by the driver. Nothing else
about scanning changes.

Unsigned experiment draft; archive identity is non-certifying.
---
 drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt6797/mac.c | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt6797/mac.c b/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt6797/mac.c
index 60e3af193..4e9bd4069 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt6797/mac.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt6797/mac.c
@@ -2275,7 +2275,13 @@ int mt6797_mac_register(struct device *dev, struct mt6797_hif *hif,
 	init_completion(&mac->join_credit_done);
 	init_completion(&mac->join_frame_done);
 	hw->wiphy->max_scan_ssids = 0;
-	hw->wiphy->max_scan_ie_len = 0;
+	/* Userspace may attach elements to a scan request (the standard
+	 * supplicant always does); with a hw_scan operation mac80211 keeps a
+	 * zero limit, and cfg80211 would then reject every such request. The
+	 * one admitted scan is passive and transmits no probe request, so the
+	 * elements are accepted here and never read; mac80211 subtracts its own.
+	 */
+	hw->wiphy->max_scan_ie_len = 256;
 	ieee80211_hw_set(hw, SIGNAL_DBM);
 #endif
 	SET_IEEE80211_DEV(hw, dev);
